{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793269219,"nonce":"yC5QvEujGUxIN4dPk+TRguxIqLc=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABa3HTXA","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/190","origin":"github"},"title":"lu(backend='pardiso') raises on a cover-space operator with SIMP void: tiny pivots are perturbed; Jacobi scaling fixes it","message":"`jno.solve.lu(backend=\"pardiso\")` raises on a `space=\"cover\"` elasticity operator as soon as the stiffness has SIMP void in it (`E = 1e-6` against `E = 1` for solid). The same operator at uniform density solves, P1 with the same contrast solves, and `backend=\"host\"` (SuperLU) solves the failing case. Symmetric Jacobi scaling before the factorisation fixes it. So PARDISO is currently unusable for a cover space in a density-based design loop. The guard does its job here: it raises instead of returning a wrong field.\n\nFound in a 3-D SIMP stress design loop on a cover space. Reproduced on `main` (7ba52b5c).\n\n### Reproduction\n\nA cantilever box `[0,2]x[0,1]x[0,1]` clamped at `x = 0`, loaded on `x = 2`, with a nodal density. The case \"half void\" puts `rho = 1` for `y \u003e 0.5` and `rho = 1e-3` elsewhere.\n\n```python\nimport jax\njax.config.update(\"jax_enable_x64\", True)\nimport jax.numpy as jnp, numpy as np\nfrom jax.experimental import sparse as jsp\nimport jno\n\nLAM, MU = 0.3 / (1.3 * 0.4), 1 / 2.6\n\ndef operator(space, rho_of_xyz, size=0.1):\n    d = jno.shape.box(0, 0, 0, 2, 1, 1, size=size).domain()\n    d.tag(\"clamp\", lambda x, y, z: x \u003c 1e-9)\n    d.tag(\"tip\", lambda x, y, z: x \u003e 2 - 1e-9)\n    kw = {\"space\": \"cover\"} if space == \"cover\" else {}\n    u, phi = d.fem_symbols(value_shape=(3,), **kw)\n    _r, s = d.fem_symbols(names=(\"r\", \"s\"))  # nodal density\n    rho = jno.np.parameter(s, name=\"rho\")\n    xi, yi, zi, _ = d.variable(\"interior\", split=True)\n    xc, yc, zc, _ = d.variable(\"clamp\", split=True)\n    xp, yp, zp, _ = d.variable(\"tip\", split=True)\n    eu, ep = jno.np.symgrad(u, [xi, yi, zi]), jno.np.symgrad(phi, [xi, yi, zi])\n    E = 1e-6 + rho**3 * (1 - 1e-6)\n    fem = jno.fem([\n        E * (LAM * jno.np.trace(eu) * jno.np.trace(ep) + 2 * MU * jno.np.inner(eu, ep, n_contract=2)),\n        u(xc, yc, zc) - (0.0, 0.0, 0.0),\n        -1.0 * jno.np.inner(jnp.array([0.0, 0.0, -1.0]), phi.bind(x=xp, y=yp, z=zp), n_contract=1),\n    ])\n    pts = np.asarray(d.mesh.points)\n    A, b = fem.operator.evaluate({\"rho\": jnp.asarray(rho_of_xyz(pts))})\n    return A, jnp.asarray(b).reshape(-1)\n\nA, b = operator(\"cover\", lambda p: np.where(p[:, 1] \u003e 0.5, 1.0, 1e-3))\njno.solve.lu(backend=\"pardiso\")(A, b)   # RuntimeError: MKL PARDISO factorized a SINGULAR operator ...\n```\n\n| space | DOFs | density | `pardiso` | `host` (SuperLU) | `pardiso`, Jacobi-scaled |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | 6,327 | uniform 0.2 | residual 8e-13 | 2e-12 | 9e-13 |\n| P1 | 6,327 | half void | 1e-12 | 2e-12 | 1e-12 |\n| cover | 25,308 | uniform 0.2 | 2e-12 | 3e-12 | 2e-12 |\n| cover | 25,308 | **half void** | **raises: 180 perturbed pivots, residual 4.5e+06** | 9e-09 | 4e-09 |\n\nThe residuals are relative, `|Ax - b| / |b|`. On the same box at `size=0.05` (154,668 cover DOFs) it is 808 perturbed pivots, with residual up to 3e+05.\n\n### Likely cause\n\nThe symmetric-indefinite factorisation (`mtype = -2`) perturbs every pivot below `1e-8 * |A|` (default `iparm(10) = 8`). A cover DOF in void has a diagonal around 1e-6 of a solid one. Its cover functions also overlap strongly with the nodal ones on the same element. Presumably that combination puts its pivot under the threshold. A P1 DOF at the same contrast stays above it, which matches the table.\n\n### Workaround (measured)\n\n```python\ndef jacobi_scaled(A, b):\n    \"\"\"x = S (S A S)^-1 S b with S = diag(A)^-1/2.\"\"\"\n    r, c = A.indices[:, 0], A.indices[:, 1]\n    d = jnp.zeros(A.shape[0], A.data.dtype).at[r].add(jnp.where(r == c, A.data, 0.0))\n    s = jax.lax.stop_gradient(1.0 / jnp.sqrt(d))  # x = A^-1 b for ANY positive S\n    As = jsp.BCOO((A.data * (s[r] * s[c]), A.indices), shape=A.shape)\n    return s * jno.solve.lu(backend=\"pardiso\")(As, s * b)\n```\n\nTwo details matter:\n\n- **Exact symmetry.** `s_i * s_j` is formed first so the scaled matrix stays EXACTLY symmetric. `(a * s_i) * s_j` differs from its transpose by one ulp, which flips the backend's exact symmetry detection to the nonsymmetric path.\n- **Gradients.** `S` carries no derivative, since `A^-1 b` does not depend on it. Holding it out of the gradient gives design sensitivities that match central finite differences to 1e-6 for P1 and cover, the same as the unscaled and SuperLU paths.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nScale inside the backend, in the host callback: scale the values, factor, solve, unscale. The JAX-side contract and the differentiability stay as they are, and every PARDISO user gets it. Untested alternatives:\n\n- MKL's own scaling with weighted matching for symmetric indefinite matrices (`iparm(11) = 1`, `iparm(13) = 1`);\n- a smaller perturbation (`iparm(10) = 13`);\n- `mtype = 2` (Cholesky) when the operator is known SPD.\n\n### Related, from reading the code\n\n`_pardiso_check_factorization` only computes the residual when `iparm(14)` (perturbed pivots) is nonzero. A non-finite solution from a factorisation that perturbed nothing would therefore pass unchecked: `rel \u003e tol` is also False for NaN. A non-finite solution came back once in the scaled setting above and was not reproduced, so this is a guard gap rather than a confirmed failure.","files":null}]}